::PC_U06_L05_Q01::
Question ID: PC_U06_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
What is a geometric series? Use the same lesson method.
{
=A. The sum of terms from a geometric sequence#Correct. A geometric series adds terms from a geometric sequence.
~B. A list that adds the same amount each time#Incorrect. Adding the same amount describes an arithmetic sequence.
~C. Only the common ratio#Incorrect. The common ratio helps define the pattern, but it is not the series.
~D. The first and last terms only#Incorrect. A series includes all terms being summed.
}
::PC_U06_L05_Q02::
Question ID: PC_U06_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
Find the sum of the first 4 terms of 3, 6, 12, 24, ... Use the same lesson method.
{
~A. 24#Incorrect. 24 is the fourth term, not the sum.
~B. 48#Incorrect. This doubles the last term rather than adding all terms.
~C. 90#Incorrect. This overcounts the series.
=D. 45#Correct. 3 + 6 + 12 + 24 = 45.
}
::PC_U06_L05_Q03::
Question ID: PC_U06_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
When does an infinite geometric series have a finite sum? Use the same lesson method.
{
~A. When the first term is 0 only#Incorrect. A first term of 0 is not the general convergence condition.
~B. Every infinite geometric series has a finite sum#Incorrect. Many infinite geometric series do not converge.
=C. When |r| is less than 1#Correct. The terms shrink toward 0 when |r| < 1.
~D. When r is greater than 1#Incorrect. If r is greater than 1, the terms grow.
}
::PC_U06_L05_Q04::
Question ID: PC_U06_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
Find the infinite sum of 10 + 5 + 2.5 + ... Use the same lesson method.
{
~A. No finite sum#Incorrect. Since |r| < 1, the series has a finite sum.
=B. 20#Correct. a1 = 10 and r = 1/2, so S = 10/(1 - 1/2) = 20.
~C. 17.5#Incorrect. This adds only the first three visible terms.
~D. 10#Incorrect. This is only the first term.
}
::PC_U06_L05_Q05::
Question ID: PC_U06_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.PC.SEQ.4
Question:
Why does 4 + 8 + 16 + ... not have a finite infinite sum? Use the same lesson method.
{
=A. The common ratio is 2, so the terms grow#Correct. Since |2| is not less than 1, the infinite sum does not converge.
~B. The first term is even#Incorrect. Being even does not determine convergence.
~C. It has too few terms#Incorrect. The dots mean the series continues forever.
~D. The ratio is 1/2#Incorrect. The ratio is 2, not 1/2.
}
